Deep Dive: How We Detect Sarcasm (Without Transformers)
1. The Core Problem
Sarcasm is unique because it is often a contradiction between content (what is said) and intent (what is meant).
Literal: "I love this weather." (It is sunny.)
Sarcastic: "I love this weather." (It is a hurricane.)
Since we cannot use Transformers (which read "context" like a human does), we have to rely on lexical cues (words) and structural cues (punctuation/patterns). This drives every decision in our environment.



2. The Preprocessing (src/utils.py)


The Code
text = re.sub(f"([{string.punctuation}])", r" \1 ", text)   


The "Why"
Standard data cleaning usually strips punctuation to make text "clean." For sarcasm, this is fatal.
"Great." is neutral.
"Great!!!" is likely enthusiastic or sarcastic.
"Great..." implies disappointment.
What we did: Instead of deleting punctuation, we isolated it. We put spaces around it.
Input: Great!!!
Output: great ! ! !
The Effect: Now, when the model looks at the data, it sees "!" not just as a character, but as a distinct "word" (token). If the model sees three "!" tokens in a row, it can learn that this pattern correlates with sarcasm.



3. Feature Engineering: TF-IDF (train_model.py)
The Code
TfidfVectorizer(ngram_range=(1, 2), min_df=2, ...)


The "Why"
Computers can't read English; they only read numbers. We need to convert ['great', 'job'] into a math vector. We used TF-IDF (Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency).
The Analogy:
Imagine you are trying to identify a document about "Sarcasm."
The word "the" appears 100 times. It has high frequency (TF) but it appears in every document, so it has low importance (IDF). Score: Low.
The word "Duh" appears 2 times, but only in sarcastic documents. It has high importance. Score: High.
What are N-Grams? (ngram_range=(1, 2))
Unigram (1): "Good", "Luck". The model sees these as separate atoms.
Bigram (2): "Good Luck". The model sees this as a specific phrase.
Why we need Bigrams: Sarcasm often lives in pairs. "Yeah" is neutral. "Yeah right" is almost always sarcastic. By using (1, 2), we let the model see both individual words and pairs.


4. The "Brains" of the Operation (The Ensemble)
We didn't trust just one algorithm. We built a Voting Ensemble. Think of this as a jury of three distinct experts trying to reach a verdict.
Juror 1: Logistic Regression (The Statistician)
What it does: It draws a straight line through the data. It calculates a specific "sarcasm weight" for every word.
Strength: It is incredibly fast and interpretable. It knows that the word "obviously" has a +5 sarcasm score and "sincerely" has a -5 score.
Weakness: It struggles with complexity. It assumes relationships are simple and linear.
Juror 2: Random Forest (The Crowd)
What it does: It builds hundreds of "Decision Trees." A decision tree asks a series of Yes/No questions (e.g., "Does it have an exclamation mark?" -> "Yes" -> "Does it say 'genius'?" -> "Yes" -> "Sarcastic").
Strength: It captures non-linear patterns. It can learn that "fine" is only sarcastic if it is followed by "...".
Weakness: It can get obsessed with memorizing the training data (overfitting).
Juror 3: Gradient Boosting (The Specialist)
What it does: It also uses decision trees, but in a sequence. It builds a weak tree, sees what it got wrong, and builds a new tree specifically to fix the errors of the first one.
Strength: It is very good at catching the "hard" cases that the other two miss.
The Foreman: Soft Voting Classifier
The Method: We used voting='soft'.
Hard Voting: Jurors just say "Guilty" (1) or "Innocent" (0). Majority wins.
Soft Voting: Jurors give a probability.
Logistic Regression: "I'm 51% sure it's sarcasm."
Random Forest: "I'm 90% sure it's sarcasm."
Gradient Boosting: "I'm 80% sure it's sarcasm."
Average: ~74%. Verdict: Sarcastic.
Why Soft? It captures the confidence of the models, which usually leads to higher accuracy.

5. The Pipeline (Pipeline([...]))
The Concept
In our code:
pipeline = Pipeline([
    ('tfidf', vectorizer),
    ('classifier', ensemble)
])


The Explanation:
Imagine you have a machine in a factory. Raw material (text) goes in one end. It must be washed (Preprocessed) and cut into shapes (Vectorized) before it can be assembled (Classified).
If we didn't use a Pipeline, we would have to manually wash and cut the test data exactly the same way we did the training data every single time. By wrapping them in a Pipeline, we create a single object that says: "Whatever text you give me, I will automatically clean it, vectorize it, and classify it."
This is why our predict_sarcasm.py is so short! It just loads the pipeline and feeds it raw text. The pipeline remembers exactly how to clean and count the words from the training phase.


6. Training vs. Inference
Training (train_model.py)
The Goal: To learn the weights.
The Process: The model looks at the text and the answers (0 or 1). It adjusts its internal math until it gets as many answers right as possible.
The Output: A .pkl file. This is effectively a "brain freeze." We save the state of the model at its smartest moment.
Inference (predict_sarcasm.py)
The Goal: To take a test.
The Process: We load the frozen .pkl brain. We feed it text without answers. It applies the math it learned earlier to give a prediction.
Crucial Detail: Notice joblib.load. This revives the saved brain. If you lose sarcasm_model.pkl, the script fails because the brain is gone.


7. Stratified Splitting


The Code
train_test_split(..., stratify=y)

The "Why"
Imagine your dataset has 90% non-sarcastic examples and 10% sarcastic ones. If you split the data randomly, you might accidentally end up with a test set that has zero sarcasm in it. Your model would look like a genius just by guessing "0" every time.
Stratify ensures fairness. It forces the split to maintain the percentage. If the main data is 10% sarcasm, the training set will be 10% sarcasm, and the test set will be 10% sarcasm. This keeps your evaluation honest.
